  "reasoning": "According to historical records from the Congressional Research Service (CRS) and analyses by organizations such as the Pew Research Center, the last time Congress enacted all regular appropriations bills prior to the start of the fiscal year was for Fiscal Year 1997 (which began on October 1, 1996). Under the Congressional Budget Act of 1974, Congress is intended to pass all 12 regular appropriations bills by the October 1 deadline. Since FY 1997, Congress has consistently relied on continuing resolutions (stopgap spending measures) or omnibus packages passed well after the start of the fiscal year to fully fund the federal government.",
